====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  MATCHED-SHOT ROW is the honest one: twirl at 65536 shots/circuit spends
  1.57e+06 total shots against fit()'s 1.31e+06 - within 1.2x.

    circuits        24  vs    160      6.7x fewer
    mean rel    0.0682  vs 1.0130      14.9x
    max  rel    0.2645  vs 5.1808      19.6x
    max |dc|    0.0140  vs 0.3017      21.6x

  AND fit() had a warm start within 0.4 of the answer while twirl had none,
  so any twirl win here is an understatement and any fit() win is inflated.

  The defensible claim is whatever the max-rel row supports at matched shots,
  stated with the warm-start asymmetry named. 'Roughly 10x the accuracy at
  6.7x fewer circuits' is withdrawn: it compared a lucky-draw mean against a
  worst case, across a 9.6x shot gap, on an easier problem.
